            ; Stone 10 set out to explain a 12.9x decode gap as DECODE
            ; ARITHMETIC and the body refused the frame twice. Our Q6_K matvec
            ; asks q6k_w(qb, flat_index) — one weight, one index, pure. ggml
            ; asks a superblock for a 4-wide slot. Same arithmetic, same
            ; weights, same machine: 7.24-10.83x apart across three real
            ; llama3.2:3b shapes and two runs. Two controlled variants said
            ; where it lives. Healing only the ARITHMETIC (division/remainder
            ; -> bit ops, our map kept) bought 2.4-2.6x. Healing only the MAP
            ; (ggml's slot, our division/remainder kept) bought 7.0-8.0x — and
            ; once the map was right, the arithmetic form was worth nothing at
            ; all: the div-arithmetic variant measured 1.06-1.37x of ggml and
            ; the bit-arithmetic one 1.16-1.46x, the same inside noise. The
            ; divisions were never the cost. They only BECAME a cost because a
            ; flat index makes the field selector g a per-weight runtime value,
            ; so q6k_pow4(g) is a runtime divisor (a real udiv per weight on a
            ; GPU with no integer divide) and a 3-way switch lands in the
            ; innermost loop. In the slot map the same expressions have
            ; loop-invariant divisors and fold to shifts: zero int_div in the
            ; inner block. Counted in AIR: 82.8 instructions and 4.25 device
            ; loads per weight ours, 24.4 and 2.125 in the slot map, 25.4 and
            ; 1.5625 ggml's — and the last residual 1.37x matches the load
            ; ratio 2.125/1.5625 = 1.36 to two digits.
            ; The cost was never in the work. It was in the GRAIN OF THE ASK:
            ; a function of one flat index cannot be told that the caller's
            ; next fifteen calls share its scale byte, so it re-loads what it
            ; already had — correctly, every single time. No call is wrong. No
            ; optimisation inside the callee can reach it.
