            ; 855 — seamtoll. Stone 13 shipped the thread map Stone 10 measured:
            ; the Q6_K matvec went from 8.28x behind llama.cpp's own kernel to
            ; 1.16x, BIT-EXACT against it (max|d| = 0.000e+00 over all 128 256
            ; rows), and the Q4_K twin shipped with it. Near-parity on the
            ; arithmetic. End-to-end moved 7.325 -> 13.197 tok/s. 1.80x, not 8x.
            ; The number that explains it: decode is 3.213 GMAC per token, which
            ; at the shipped kernel's measured 442 GMAC/s is 7.3 ms. A token
            ; takes 51.9 ms. The other 44.6 ms is spread across 396 DISPATCHES
            ; per token — 113 us each, and the profile's cheapest op (a residual
            ; add of 3072 floats, nearly pure seam) independently measures
            ; 133 us. ollama's 157.83 tok/s is 6.34 ms per token, which sits AT
            ; the arithmetic floor, not above it. So the whole remaining gap is
            ; charged not by any operation but by the JOINS BETWEEN them.
            ; What is chilling is that the arithmetic was the right thing to fix
            ; and fixing it was still not the thing. The binding constraint had
            ; already MOVED, and the measurement that sent this stone was taken
            ; before the move — taken, correctly, on the surface that used to be
            ; the cost. A component brought to parity buys only its own share.
