            ; 856 — gapmete. Stone 15 was sent to carve two GGUF quant types no
            ; published code decodes, and handed the only declaration that exists:
            ; ds4's own parse table, "nvfp4" 64 elements in 36 bytes and "q1_0"
            ; 128 in 18. Both rows are wrong about the file they ship to parse.
            ; What overruled them was not a better source — there is none — but
            ; the SPACING of the file's own contents. 1406 tensor-info records
            ; carry absolute offsets; the distance from each tensor to the next,
            ; divided by its element count, is the bytes-per-element the writer
            ; actually used. Run on types whose geometry is already known the
            ; instrument returns f32 4.0, f16 2.0, i32 4.0, iq2_xxs 0.2578125 —
            ; exactly, so it is calibrated before it is trusted. Run on the two
            ; unknown types it returns one value each with no spread across 45
            ; and 370 tensors: 34 bytes per 64, and 132 per 128. 36 is not merely
            ; unattested, it is IMPOSSIBLE — it would make one tensor overlap the
            ; next by 67 MB, and tensors do not overlap. The declaration lost to
            ; the arrangement. Nothing in the file says what the format IS; the
            ; gaps say what it CANNOT be, and that was enough to reach it.
            ; The teaching is that a placeholder is not a claim. That table row
            ; is never exercised — ds4 refuses both types before reaching it — so
            ; its wrongness had never cost anyone anything and had never been
            ; seen. An unexercised declaration is unfalsifiable, and its
            ; confidence is entirely borrowed from the code around it.
            ; Distinct from what the body carries: unispan 838 says never claim
            ; from one sample, and gapmete is where the samples come FROM when
