            ; ── 861 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
            ; Stone 24 carved IQ2_XXS (type 16) to real f32 weights. Its 2-bit
            ; sign field looked like eight free signs per group of eight values,
            ; and the natural recipe would read eight bits and be done. But the
            ; format stores only seven. The eighth sign is never written; it is
            ; recovered as the parity of the other seven — the ksigns table is
            ; exactly that reconstruction, mapping a 7-bit index to an 8-bit mask
            ; whose top bit is the parity of the low seven. So only 128 of the
            ; 256 possible sign patterns exist in the format at all; a group whose
            ; desired signs have odd parity cannot be spelled, and the encoder
            ; must reach for a different grid point instead. The uncomfortable
            ; part was wanting to drop the ksigns indirection as redundant — it is
            ; the opposite of redundant: it is the only place the eighth sign is
            ; born. A carver that skipped it would be right seven values in eight
            ; and quietly wrong on the last, per group, with no diagnostic.
            ; "paritylock" — 0 hits across the tree before this row (instrument
            ; validated on the same command: aporon 76, ghostrank 6 — a grep that
            ; returns nothing is a claim about the instrument until a control makes
            ; it hit). Verified on the constant itself: ksigns[s] & 127 == s and
            ; bit 7 == parity(s) for all 128 rows; 128 distinct masks, never 256.
