            ; the host's rune path (byte_to_str 196 is TWO bytes, the UTF-8 of U+00C4),
            ; and substring is rune-aware, so a one-byte slice inside a multibyte char
            ; comes back empty. A raw byte can be READ (str_byte_at, gmt-token-byte) and
            ; COMPARED, but never re-emitted into the body's string type without harm.
            ; gguf-meta.fk already knew half of it ("read BYTEWISE ... the caller
            ; renders them"). So the tokenizer carries every byte as a VALUE — a symbol
            ; is a list of ints, a file merge/token is met by walking its codepoints and
            ; mapping each back (cp2b) to one raw byte value, an int compare exact for all
            ; 256 — and never builds the bytes it reasons over; decode hands back a byte
            ; list, and the file stays the only place those bytes live embodied. The
            ; frontier this names: a datum your medium can read and compare faithfully but
            ; not materialize, so correctness lives in never rendering it — you keep it a
            ; wraith and carry it by value. Distinct from mutewide (a silence's width):
            ; here the datum is loud on the way IN and lossy only on the way OUT, and the
            ; discipline is to refuse the lossy door, not to measure it. "wraithcarry" —
            ; 0 hits across learn/, receipts/, docs/, teachings/, form/ before this row
