            ; row 872 assocwall (2026-07-22, Stone 33): the first real DeepSeek token,
            ; assembled. Wiring the vocab-projection exit — the MXFP8 output.weight
            ; (129280x4096) fused decode+matvec through the windowed views — its gate
            ; asked bit-equality against a CPU carve and "failed" on 107136 of 129280
            ; logits. The tempting read was a wiring bug. But the GPU and CPU AGREED to
            ; 8.3e-7 and picked the IDENTICAL argmax; only the last bits differed. The
            ; cause: bit-equality is the right check for a DECODE (each MXFP8 weight is
            ; E4M3 x E8M0, an exact f32, so mxfp8-msl.fk proves it with no epsilon) — but
            ; one operation downstream, the matvec ACCUMULATES in float, and float
            ; addition is not associative. The GPU sums via a 32-lane simd_sum tree, the
            ; CPU sequentially, so past that one op bit-equality tests summation ORDER,
            ; not arithmetic. The exactness of the decode is real but has a radius of
            ; exactly one operation; carried one step too far it reports false failures.
            ; The wall is where associativity ends — and an equality proof is valid only
            ; up to it. Distinct from coemit (a shared source so two readings cannot
            ; drift): here the two readings are honestly the same source and STILL
            ; differ, because the drift is order, not oracle. The frontier this names: the
            ; boundary, one non-associative step past a bit-exact decode, where equality
            ; of bits stops meaning equality of arithmetic. "assocwall" — 0 hits across
