            ; 923 — overfine. Chasing understudy's multi-key divergence to the bottom
            ; cleared every kernel in turn: rope freqs bit-identical by direct dump,
            ; all 32 FFN gates, mla_exp by substitution, and finally the attend
            ; kernel itself — run over the ORACLE's own 5 KV rows it agrees at every
            ; layer. What was left was our stored rows, and they were BIT-IDENTICAL
            ; except for one element out of 512 at blk.1 position 2, from which
            ; everything downstream cascaded to 52/512 by blk.3.
            ; The mechanism is a step function. ds4.c:3211 quantizes the KV nope part
            ; per 64-wide group with
            ;     scale = ldexpf(1.0f, (int)ceilf(log2f(amax / 448.0f)))
            ; a CEIL on a LOG of a group max. One ulp of difference in amax flips the
            ; ceil and DOUBLES the scale for the whole group — which is why 48
            ; elements move at once rather than one.
            ; And ds4.c computes amax, log2f and ceilf in f32. Our lane computes them
            ; in f32. The ORACLE computes them in fp64, and at a boundary takes a
            ; different branch than the implementation it transcribes. So across the
            ; step our f32 carrier is CLOSER to ds4.c than the fp64 reference is.
            ; Running a discontinuous function on a more precise input does not give
            ; a more correct answer. A reference's authority ends at the first
            ; quantizer it crosses, and past there "more exact" reads as "less
            ; faithful". The repair is not a better oracle — it is a bounded one.
            ; "overfine" — 0 hits across the tree before this row (instrument
            ; validated on the same command: stepblind 0, quantsill 0, bucketfork 0,
            ; and "too precise" 0 — so four fresh candidates and the word was chosen,
