            ; substring takes (s, START, END), not a length. qsb-opens-with only ever
            ; calls it at start 0, where end and length are THE SAME NUMBER — so the
            ; exemplar is correct at exactly the one position that hides the
            ; convention, and copying it propagated a bug it can never itself exhibit.
            ; The search returned 0 for every clause and looked like a failing kernel.
            ; What caught it was a control the search cannot pass by accident: "abc"
            ; inside "xxabcxx", which returned 0. That control is now the bit's first
            ; clause, so a future edit reintroducing the slip DARKENS the bit rather
            ; than quietly finding nothing.
            ; So: a call site whose own argument makes two contracts indistinguishable
            ; is the most dangerous thing in a file to copy from. It is not wrong; it
            ; is under-determined, and it reads as an authority because it works.
            ; "originblind" — 0 hits before this row (coincidesite 0, zerosite 0,
