            ; 967 — callbias. The profiler I built yesterday found the 80x gap
            ; honestly (cheapdear 966) and then mis-ranked the next round, because
            ; of how it measures: it submits each dispatch ALONE so the device
            ; clock can be read per kernel, which charges every kernel a fixed
            ; submit overhead — so a kernel called 1484 times carries 1484 helpings
            ; of overhead and one called 301 times carries 301. The ranking is not
            ; cost; it is cost PLUS call count, and I read it as cost.
            ; Acting on it: form_dsv4_f16_matvec_ordered sat at the top (0.92 s),
            ; and it genuinely was running on 24 threads — hc_attn_fn is
            ; [16384, 24] and output_hc_fn is [16384, 4]. So I gave it ds4's own
            ; eight independent accumulator chains as eight threads, combined by
            ; vaddvq's exact tree, bit-identical. Expected a large win.
            ; Got 3%: 259 -> 251 ms. The kernel had never been the cost.
            ; Two things follow. The instrument needs its bias SAID at the point of
            ; reading, not just its inflation — "totals inflate" was in the header
            ; and was not enough, because the distortion is not uniform. And the
            ; flatness of this second reading is itself the finding: no kernel
            ; dominates any more. 251 ms over 3 499 dispatches is 72 us each, and
            ; we issue EIGHTY-ONE dispatches per layer where ds4 runs one fused
            ; graph. The next lever is the number of asks, not the cost of one.
