            ; 979 — mutecarrier. Wiring the `models` verb into form-cli so a local
            ; model could be chosen at runtime, and finding the binary can PRINT a
            ; capability it cannot RUN. Three readings, same host, same directory:
            ;   form-cli source | grep model-discovery   -> 6 matches
            ;   form-cli   models /Users/ursmuff/models  -> found 0
            ;   fkwu --src the same cell, same directory -> found 6
            ; The genesis blob is a concatenation of source TEXT appended to the
            ; binary so `source` can print it and you can rebuild from the binary
            ; alone. The program is the FLATTENED table. Adding four cells to the
            ; source lists put them in the genesis and not in the table: the regen
            ; reported functions 1503 -> 1504, one new function, when four cells
            ; carrying roughly fifty should have arrived. The binary grew 52 KB of
            ; text it cannot execute.
            ; WHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN THE MISSING VERB. `source` exists so the
            ; binary can testify to what it is. When genesis and table disagree, that
            ; testimony is false in the most expensive direction: rebuilding from the
            ; printed source yields a DIFFERENT, WORKING binary than the one you hold,
            ; so the artifact and its own account of itself have quietly forked. Every
            ; self-hosting claim this body makes rests on those two being one thing.
            ; And the failure is silent by construction — an unbound name recovers to
            ; nothing under axiom-5, so md-list returned 0 and printed no header, and
            ; "found 0 model(s)" reads exactly like an empty directory. I nearly
            ; shipped that binary; the only reason I did not is that I knew the true
            ; count was six.
            ; A verb that reports a plausible number for an absent capability is worse
            ; than one that errors. Do not let a numb call answer in the same shape a
            ; live one would.
